CjyqbD48t4hcDF9kxxRvQC10aTwo wheels to freedom :bthe story of a young Jew, wartime resistance, and a daring escape /cArthur J. Magida30aStory of a young Jew, wartime resistance, and a daring escape3 a2 wheels to freedom  aFirst Pegasus Books cloth edition 1aNew York :bPegasus Books,c2024 4c©2024  axxii, 277 pages, 16 unnumbered leaves of plates :billustrations (some color), map ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 251-277)0 apart 1. The wanderers: "You better go back" ; "In earlier times, they would have burned us" ; Hitchhiking on the Autobahn ; "This business could have gone very badly" ; "Nothing ever happened except one kiss" -- part 2. "It'll be our turn one day": The butter conspiracy ; An act of lunacy ; "Do not shed too many tears" ; A night on the town ; The kindest Nazi Fanja ever met ; A brainless Hitler and a stupid joke ; He took the job ; The yard sale of the century ; The reluctant forger -- part 3. Into the dark: "Better than dueling with sabers" ; "You shall be the emperor of my soul" ; Cioma, the German nobleman ; Cioma's comrade ; The tide turns ; "Don't show your face in public" -- part 4. The exodus: "Simple is what you are, Sch̲nhaus" ; Hiding, loving, fleeing ; "The lifeboat is full" ; The great balancing ; Pigs to avoid ; "I'm crossing my fingers for you, Sch̲nhaus" ; "I only want to warn you" ; No more hopeless plans -- Postscript: "I know you. I know you very well."  aThough Cioma Schonhaus was only 11 years old when the Nazis first came to power, his cleverness and resourcefulness eventually made him an unlikely hero and bon vivant. As a young adult staying one step ahead of the S.S., Cioma would dine in swanky restaurants and frequent trendy bars and have plenty of romances--all while sabotaging weapons in the munitions factory where he worked. He even bought a sailboat and taught himself how to sail. These hijinks never distracted Cioma from a deeper mission. Trained as an artist, Cioma's masterfully forged fake IDs ensured that several hundred Jews survived the war. When he learned the Gestapo was closing in on him, Cioma masterminded a singularly daring escape: spending a month biking to Switzerland, he became the only person to cycle his way out of the Third Reich10aSchönhaus, Cioma,d1922-2015.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no2006014885 0aJewish artistszGermanyxHistoryy20th centuryvBiography. 0aWorld War, 1939-1945xJewsvBiography. 0aJewszGermanyxHistoryy20th centuryvBiography. 0aForgeryzGermanyxHistoryy20th century. 0aHolocaust, Jewish (1939-1945)zGermany.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008105693 0aIdentification cardsxForgerieszGermanyxHistoryy20th century. 0aJewsxPersecutionszGermanyxHistoryy20th century.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2009127800 0aEscapeszGermanyxHistoryy20th century. 0aHolocaust, Jewish (1939-1945)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85061515 0aIdentification cardsxForgerieszGermany. 0aJewsxPersecutionszGermany.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85070468 0aWorld War, 1939-1945.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85148273 6aFaux0(CaQQLa)201-0062227zAllemagne0(CaQQLa)201-0392817xHistoire0(CaQQLa)201-0378892y20e siècle.0(CaQQLa)201-0378892 6aHolocauste, 1939-1945.0(CaQQLa)201-0011801 6aCartes d'identité0(CaQQLa)201-0221022xFaux0(CaQQLa)201-0221022zAllemagne.0(CaQQLa)201-0392817 6aJuifsxPersécutionszAllemagne.0(CaQQLa)201-0171276 6aGuerre mondiale, 1939-1945.0(CaQQLa)201-0056582 6aArtistes juifs0(CaQQLa)201-0143092zAllemagne0(CaQQLa)201-0392817xHistoire0(CaQQLa)201-0378892y20e siècle0(CaQQLa)201-0378892vBiographies.0(CaQQLa)201-0378258 6aGuerre mondiale, 1939-19450(CaQQLa)201-0011803xJuifs0(CaQQLa)201-0011803vBiographies.0(CaQQLa)201-0378258 6aHolocauste, 1939-19450(CaQQLa)201-0011801zAllemagne.0(CaQQLa)201-0392817 6aCartes d'identité0(CaQQLa)201-0221022xFaux0(CaQQLa)201-0221022zAllemagne0(CaQQLa)201-0392817xHistoire0(CaQQLa)201-0378892y20e siècle.0(CaQQLa)201-0378892 6aJuifs0(CaQQLa)201-0171276xPersécutions0(CaQQLa)201-0171276zAllemagne0(CaQQLa)201-0171276xHistoire0(CaQQLa)201-0378892y20e siècle.0(CaQQLa)201-0378892 6aÉvasions0(CaQQLa)201-0089650zAllemagne0(CaQQLa)201-0392817xHistoire0(CaQQLa)201-0378892y20e siècle.0(CaQQLa)201-0378892 7aBiographies.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026049 7aAutobiographies.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026047  aGOBI  aGreen StacksbGERMANIC-SULc40032463283dbooke24.26g325011h188847kUSDm0.00q1rCan circulatetApproval planuVendor order reference numbervYANKEE-SUL  a36105243646879ffi49e90331-999f-4d5e-bf17-c3e840d2158cs3ffb0fa2-f194-4635-8bf6-582ed47622f905757nab a2200709ui in0000030503520250403135139.0m||||go|||||||||||cr mn|---uuuuu150331s2010||||xx || | |||||||   ||eng|d  als3088  aNYHLSbengerdacNYHLSdCstdUtOrBLW00ae-flux Journal #16 1a[Place of publication not identified] :be-flux,c2010  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 ae-flux journal ;v16  aArchived and cataloged by Library Stack  aUnrestricted online access  a"Things would be much simpler if there existed a consistent means of evaluating art's capacity to provide a concrete value for people. It's a problem to which capital provides the most immediate solution-beyond the mundane routine of the art market, Brandeis University's (ongoing) attempt to close their Rose Art Museum and liquidate its entire collection stands as a particularly unfortunate example of how a priceless collection of art, given the right circumstances (total financial meltdown), still finds its price. One is also reminded of the tragic decision by Middlesex University to close its renowned philosophy department in order to cut costs-in spite of the fact that the department's meager staff of only six professors generate 5% of the large university's research revenue, as Peter Osborne, head of the Centre for Research in Modern European Philosophy at Middlesex, has noted... Editorial Julieta Aranda, Brian Kuan Wood, Anton Vidokle The Unthinkable Community Paul Chan Music-Immateriality-Value Diedrich Diederichsen The Good of Work Liam Gillick Wu Yongfang, the Hunger Artist Hu Fang Michael Jackson Died for No Reason (and the Vampire that is His Life) Bilal Khbeiz Art and Thingness, Part III: The Heart of the Thing is the Thing We Don't Know Sven Lütticken Innovative Forms of Archives, Part Two: IRWIN's East Art Map and Tamás St. Auby's Portable Intelligence Increase Museum Nataša Petrešin-Bachelez (Jena Revisited) Ten Tentative Tenets Dieter Roelstraete Art Without Artists? Anton Vidokle An Open Letter to Clifford Irving"--cprovided by distributor  aStandard Copyright10aArchives.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500691310aArt and music.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500796710aArt criticism.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500798910aHistoriography.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8506121110aHousing.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8506260310aImperialism.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8506462810aMuseums--Curatorship.10aPerformance art.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8509980910aPolitical art.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh201800299717aArchives.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008140300http://id.worldcat.org/fast/81403017aArt and music.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008154130http://id.worldcat.org/fast/81541317aArt criticism.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008154920http://id.worldcat.org/fast/81549217aColonies.2fast0(OCoLC)fst009681260http://id.worldcat.org/fast/86845617aCuratorship.2fast0(OCoLC)fst010301490http://id.worldcat.org/fast/189339717aHistoriography.2fast0(O