-1950.tArlesiana.pÈ la solita storia.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8012535912aPuccini, Giacomo,d1858-1924.tFanciulla del West.pCh'ella mi creda libero e lontano.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8102246612aLehár, Franz,d1870-1948.tLand des Lächelns.pDein ist mein ganzes Herz.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9503289612aPuccini, Giacomo,d1858-1924.tTosca.pRecondita armonia.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8115017212aRossini, Gioacchino,d1792-1868.tBarbiere di Siviglia.pEcco ridente in cielo.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8536787012aGiordano, Umberto,d1867-1948.tFedora.pAmor ti vieta.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8102245812aPuccini, Giacomo,d1858-1924.tBohème.pChe gelida manina.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8013174312aMascagni, Pietro,d1863-1945.tAmico Fritz.pO amore, o bella luce del core.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9102834112aGiordano, Umberto,d1867-1948.tAndrea Chénier.pCome un bel dì di maggio.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8404674712aPuccini, Giacomo,d1858-1924.tTosca.pE lucevan le stelle.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8111913012aOffenbach, Jacques,d1819-1880.tBelle Hélène.pJugement de Paris.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no97007112  aRobert Baxter collection  aSUL/MUSIC/catalogedby/Flourish-CAM/202411  aSUL/MUSIC/gift/kevkishi/20241205/Baxter  b36105234619588cMCD 74524n1lMusic PAGE-MU (MUS-PAGE-MU)  aC0bSTFffs2ad01b49-23eb-4de7-9c43-6b3b216b56dei4edb6ead-7cbc-469d-91a4-176f2978454508814nam a2201285ui in0000030069820250403135150.0m||||go|||||||||||cr mn|---uuuuu170205s2016||||xx ||||go|||||||| ||eng|d  als8045  aNYHLSbengerdacNYHLSdCstdUtOrBLW04aThe Zoology Lesson 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bThe Volume Project,c2016  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aUnfold ;v2  aArchived and cataloged by Library Stack  aUnrestricted online access  a"The Literal Intimacies of Zoology: Reading Through the Folders of Colonial-Science Sara Giannini in conversation with Anna-Sophie Springer and Etienne Turpin Sara Giannini: First of all, I would like to thank you for being a part of Unfold. I am thrilled to delve into your selection for Unfold#2 in only a few days. It is both exciting and intimidating because all the materials I have organized for the project will be so closely read and appropriated by your intervention. Since the launch of Unfold in September 2015, I have been going back to the problem of legibility and its ties to collecting. Etymologically, reading and collecting ["legere"/"colligere"] stem from intimately gathering together. I think that the practice of reading is the essential comportment of the project on several different levels: from Benjamin's citing labour in The Arcades Project to my re-reading of it through the artistic contributions which repurpose projects for the specific space of the folder, and from the further readings which I commissioned, to those enlivened by the reader/viewer/user. With these remarks in mind, I wonder how you read Unfold#1? Anna-Sophie Springer & Etienne Turpin: We read as we would in any other space, whether physical or digital, which is to say, promiscuously. If Walter Benjamin saw himself as a rag-picker in the history of the catastrophe called capitalism, we are, similarly, just scavenging through the ruined debris of the ivory tower, which is not without certain charms for the collector. Unfold presents a form that aligns with our style of inquiry, and we were excited to present a collection that can unfold for your readers as they work through The Lesson of Zoology...  "--cprovided by distributor  aStandard Copyright10aArchives.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500691310aEcology.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8504075210aImperialism.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8506462810aLibrary science.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8507672317aArchives.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008140300http://id.worldcat.org/fast/81403017aColonies.2fast0(OCoLC)fst009681260http://id.worldcat.org/fast/86845617aEcology.2fast0(OCoLC)fst009014760http://id.worldcat.org/fast/90147617aLibrary science.2fast0(OCoLC)fst009979160http://id.worldcat.org/fast/997916 7aArt.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027218 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aArt.2fast0(OCoLC)fst014237020http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1423702 7aDatabases.2fast0(OCoLC)fst014116430http://id.worldcat.org/fast/141164317aImperialism.2fast0(OCoLC)fst009681260http://id.worldcat.org/fast/96812617aWorld politics.2fast0(OCoLC)fst011813810http://id.worldcat.org/fast/11813811 a, Pseudo-Galen,econtributor.1 aAlbert, Bruce,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n906598581 aBallard, J. G.,econtributor.1 aBenjamin, Walter,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n940761061 aBlaschka, Leopold,econtributor.1 aBlaschka, Rudolph,econtributor.1 aBourgery, Jean Baptiste Marc,econtributor.1 aBowker, C. Geoffrey,econtributor.1 aBrecht, Bertolt,econtributor.1 aBurnett, Graham D.,econtributor.1 aCaillois, Roger,econtributor.1 aCioran, E. M.,econtributor.1 aCohen, Deborah R.,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n868563421 aCuvier, Georges,econtributor.1 aDaston, Lorraine,econtributor.1 aDavis, Heather,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n20041214851 aDe Landa, Manuel,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n882946721 aDeleuze, Gilles,econtributor.1 aD'Holbach, Baron,econtributor.1 aDick, Phillip K.,econtributor.1 aDoyle, Arthur Conan,econtributor.1 aEnloe, Linda J.,econtributor.1 aFoucault, Michel,econtributor.1 aGerrard, John,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n85294