,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nr950112261 aRich, Jon,econtributor.1 aSteyerl, Hito,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20050144731 aTan, Pelin,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20090886141 aVidokle, Anton,eeditor,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20061296551 aWeizman, Eyal,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nb980652781 aWood, Brian Kuan,eeditor,econtributor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nb20110095182 aLibrary Stack,edistributor.2 aMetahaven,eContributor. 0aE-flux journal (Series) ;v38.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201116589040zAvailable to Stanford-affiliated users.uhttps://www.librarystack.org/e-flux-journal-38/yLibrary StackxProvider: Library StackxpurchasedxeLoaderURLxls4zTo create lists or check out books that are not open access, users must create a Library Stack account.xls284142uhttps://www.librarystack.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/cover38-480x639.pngzCover Image Preview  aeLoaderRecord xls4ffi59bf6671-c42a-4419-9a0b-2f656139990ese0f016ae-1d8d-4e82-8347-41af09483a4d06527nam a2200793ui in0000030174820250403135227.0a||||go|||||||||||cr mn|---uuuuu221228s2022||||xx ||||go|||||||| u|eng|d  als115414  aNYHLSbengerdacNYHLSdCstdUtOrBLW00aEconomies of Virtue: The Circulation of 'Ethics' in AI 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bInstitute of Network Cultures,c2022  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aTheory on Demand ;v46  aArchived and cataloged by Library Stack  aUnrestricted online access  a"AI ethics has never been far from the industries it sought to critique. While originally designed to bring values such as fairness, accountability and transparency to Big Tech and its products, the lines between Big Tech's PR initiatives and AI ethics funding has never been clear. In practice, AI ethics now operates as a means for the co-option of critics and to enable regulatory capture. It is used by corporations to create legitimacy and to further accumulate value. The result is that 'ethics' has now become a high-valued industrial commodity, and AI ethics its foundry. This anthology is a collective response to the reification of ethics into commodity forms. It explores how industry participation in 'ethical AI' research has created a new 'economy of virtue'-a massive network of actors variously situated across industry, civil society, and universities, producing and circulating ethics as a service and a product. The contributors present both critical perspectives and first-hand experiences of this economy. They address a wide range of topics including: the contradictions and personal dilemmas of working in industry-funded spaces; case studies of AI ethics in domains such as defence, facial recognition, and standards setting; critical assessments of techniques like green-washing and the manufacture of trust; and the risks and practicalities of direct action such as speaking up, organizing against and dropping out. Together, these contributions give voice to the intractable problems of co-option, capture, and complicity that plague AI ethics, and give shape to the networks and circulations defining the field."--cprovided by distributor  aCC BY-NC-SA10aAlgorithms.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500348710aArtificial intelligence.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8500818010aBiometric identification.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh200101096410aCapital movements.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8501994610aComputer networks.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8502951310aData mining.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh9700207310aMilitary-industrial complex.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8800156910aPropaganda.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh8510744317aAlgorithms.2fast0(OCoLC)